er 1\n\nHundreds:5+3=8 +1=9\n\nThousands:8+0=8\n\nTen thousands:9+6=15 \u2192 5, carryover 1\n\nHundred thousands:9+9=18 +1 (carryover) =19 \u2192 9, carryover 1\n\nMillions: the carryover 1.\n\nSo the digits are:\n\nMillions: 1\n\nHundred thousands:9\n\nTen thousands:5\n\nThousands:8\n\nHundreds:9\n\nTens:1\n\nOnes:7\n\nTherefore the total is 1,958,917.\n\nLet me confirm by another method. Let's add the numbers in parts.\n\nFirst number: 998,576\n\nSecond number: 960,341\n\nAdding them:\n\n998,576 + 960,341\n\nBreakdown:\n\n998,576 + 900,000 = 1,898,576\n\nThen add the remaining 60,341:\n\n1,898,576 + 60,000 = 1,958,576\n\nThen add 341: 1,958,576 + 341 = 1,958,917. That matches.\n\nAlternatively, use calculator-like addition:\n\nStart with 998,576\n\nAdd 960,341:\n\nAdding the thousands first: 998,000 + 960,000 = 1,958,000\n\nThen add the remaining 576 + 341 = 917\n\nTotal: 1,958,000 + 917 = 1,958,917. Yep, that works.\n\nSo the correct answer should be 1,958,917.\n\nWait, but the user's initial problem was stated as: \"What is the sum of 998576 and 960341?\" So that's exactly what I did here, and the result is 1,958,917. Therefore, I think that's the correct answer. I just need to make sure I didn't make any arithmetic errors in my head.\n\nAlternatively, to be thorough, let me add the numbers digit by digit again:\n\nFirst number: 998576\n\nSecond number: 960341\n\nAligning:\n\n      9 9 8 5 7 6\n\n    + 9 6 0 3 4 1\n\n    ----------------\n\nStarting from the right:\n\nOnes: 6+1=7\n\nTens:7+4=11 \u2192 1 carry 1\n\nHundreds:5+3=8 +1=9\n\nThousands:8+0=8\n\nTen thousands:9+6=15 \u21925 carry 1\n\nHundred thousands:9+9=18 +1=19 \u21929 carry 1\n\nMillions: carryover 1\n\nSo writing the digits: 1 (million), 9 (hundred thousand), 5 (ten thousand), 8 (thousand), 9 (hundred), 1 (ten),7 (one). So 1,958,917.\n\nYes, that's consistent. I think that's the right answer. I was confused earlier because I started thinking of a different problem, but now I corrected that. So t