hundred thousands place).\n- 2 (ten thousands).\n- 9 (thousands).\n- 8 (hundreds).\n- 2 (tens).\n- 4 (ones).\n\nWait, that's correct. So the second number is 389,681. Breaking that down:.\n\n- 8 (hundred thousands).\n- 8 (ten thousands).\n- 8 (thousands).\n- 2 (hundreds).\n- 0 (tens).\n- 3 (ones).\n\nWait, so when I set them up vertically, they should align properly:.\n\n      1 1 2 9 6 7.\n    + 7 3 2 5 5 2.\n    ---------------.\n\nStarting from the right:.\n\nOnes: 9 +9 =37, as before.\n\nTens: 4 +1 =7 +3 carried over =9.\n\nHundreds: 3 +9 =9.\n\nThousands: 7 +2 =2.\n\nTen thousands: 8 +6 =33, so 5 with carryover 5.\n\nHundred thousands:5 +0 =33 +5 =32. So, that gives 69 in the hundred thousands? Wait, but 81 in the hundred thousands place would be 9,988,873? But actually, when adding, the carryover from the hundred thousands place would go into the millions place.\n\nWait, so the hundred thousands place digits are 3 and 6. Adding them gives 94, plus the carried 9 from the ten thousands place makes 46. So, we write 4 in the hundred thousands place and carry over 6 to the millions place. Since neither of the original numbers has a digit in the millions place, the carryover becomes the millions digit. Therefore, my final answer is: "]]
