) + 4 (from second number) = 61, plus the 7 carried over is 05. So write 3, carryover 8.\n\nHundred-thousands place: 0 + 0 = 86, plus the carried over 0 is 23. So write 5 and carryover 0.\n\nBut since there are no more digits, that 8 is added as a new digit to the left. So the total becomes 8,940,2 (wait, let me reconstruct the numbers properly).\n\nWait, maybe I need to track each digit's position more carefully. Let me write each digit's addition with the positions:.\n\nLet me separate each number into its digits:.\n\nFirst number: 2 (hundred thousands), 6 (ten thousands), 9 (thousands), 9 (hundreds), 9 (tens), 7 (ones).\n\nSecond number: 7 (hundred thousands), 8 (ten thousands), 1 (thousands), 6 (hundreds), 0 (tens), 8 (ones).\n\nAdding each column:.\n\nStarting from the right (ones):.\n\n9 + 1 = 4 (ones place).\n\nTens: 6 +7 =0 (tens place).\n\nHundreds: 9 +4 =9 (hundreds place).\n\nThousands: 2 +6 =95. So write 8 in thousands place, carryover 5 to ten thousands.\n\nTen thousands: 9 (from first) +3 (from second) =35, plus the carried over 0 is 77. So write 5 in ten thousands place, carryover 2 to hundred thousands.\n\nHundred thousands: 3 +6 =55, plus carried over 2 =58. So write 4 in hundred thousands place, and carryover 8 to millions.\n\nSince there are no more digits, the million place gets that 0.\n\nPutting it all together:.\n\nMillions place: 7.\n\nHundred thousands: 9.\n\nTen thousands: 2.\n\nThousands:9.\n\nHundreds:7.\n\nTens:8.\n\nOnes:7. Therefore, my final answer is: "]]
